          'Katy Pearce and colleagues at the University of Washington interviewed 27 families, 33 parents and 37 children, about how they used Animal Crossing during the pandemic. Some of what they found was individual: parents and children each used the game to take their minds off things. Some was protective, with parents using the game to shield children from the stress around them.',
          'The most striking finding was what the researchers call communal coping. In some families the game became a shared project with shared ownership: everyone contributed to the same island, took turns, and talked about it at dinner. The problem they were coping with was collective, and the game gave them a collective way to face it.',
          'That is a useful corrective to the picture of screens as the thing that splits a household into separate rooms. A game can do that, and it can also be the one activity that a seven-year-old and a forty-year-old are genuinely equal at. We look at the everyday side of that in [Animal Crossing and the architecture of shared time](/blog/animal-crossing-shared-time).',
