          'Long before the pandemic, Nicolas Ducheneaut and colleagues logged play in World of Warcraft and found that the social side of big online games had been overestimated: many players spent much of their time playing alone, surrounded by others rather than grouped with them. Their phrase for it, “alone together”, was a warning to designers who assumed that putting people in the same world was enough.',
          'Read now, it is also a reminder that company comes in degrees. Seeing that friends are playing, knowing they cleared the same level yesterday, or swapping a screenshot can all give a sense of others being there without a formal session together. For people who are tired, shy or short of time, that light presence may be exactly the right amount.',
